Fix DMD rendering as a black rectangle on the OpenGL renderer (BW_FP32 textures).

The RED32F color format was never added to the format tables of the OpenGL
Sampler, in either CreateTexture() or UpdateTexture(). It therefore falls
through to the defaults of both ternary chains:

    const GLuint col_type = ((format == RGBA32F) || (format == RGB32F)) ? GL_FLOAT
                          : ...
                          : GL_UNSIGNED_BYTE;              // <- RED32F lands here
    const GLuint col_format = ((format == GREY8) || (format == RED16F)) ? GL_RED
                          : ...
                          : GL_RGBA;                       // <- and here

so a BW_FP32 image, whose texture is allocated as GL_R32F by glTexStorage2D,
gets uploaded with:

    glTexSubImage2D(..., GL_RGBA, GL_UNSIGNED_BYTE, data);

GL requires GL_RED/GL_FLOAT for an R32F texture and rejects the call with
INVALID_OPERATION. Nothing checks glGetError here, so the upload silently does
nothing and the texture keeps the zeroes left by glTexStorage2D. Sampling it
returns 0, and the DMD shader multiplies it out to black.

BW_FP32 is the format used for luminance DMD frames, so any display source
publishing CTLPI_DISPLAY_FORMAT_LUM32F -- PinMAME's DMD, notably -- renders as
a black rectangle: correctly sized and positioned, with a valid texture and the
right tint, but empty. Sources publishing sRGB frames (FlexDMD and friends) take
another branch of the table and are unaffected, which is what makes this look
like a ScoreView or a PinMAME problem rather than a texture upload one.

Note RED16F is present in the GL_RED list but RED32F is not, which suggests it
was simply missed when the format was introduced. col_is_linear has the same
omission: a float format is always linear.

Only the OpenGL renderer is affected, the BGFX backend has its own upload path.

Signed-off-by: Adrien Beudin <beudbeud@gmail.com>

